# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/
main.js
*.zip

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vault content must NEVER be committed. This repo is PUBLIC and holds source
# code only. `test-vault/` is the sole exception: it is synthetic by rule.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/vault/
*.vault/
**/95 Exports/
**/82 Audit/
**/80 Time/
**/75 Correspondence/
**/94 Runs/

# There is deliberately NO `!` exception to the list above — not for test-vault,
# not for anything. `75 Correspondence/` is the highest-risk folder in a real
# vault (§5.10 permits full message bodies and attachments in it), and a rule
# with no exceptions is the only kind that still holds at the end of a long day.
#
# The synthetic threads that document that contract therefore live in
# `test-vault/75 Correspondence-fixtures/`, which is a different folder name and
# so was never ignored in the first place. Drop a real thread into a folder
# actually called `75 Correspondence/` — anywhere, including under test-vault —
# and it stays out of this repo.

# Obsidian per-machine state (never useful in the repo)
**/.obsidian/workspace*
**/.obsidian/plugins/scdb-cockpit/
**/.obsidian/cache

# Machine-local secrets: absolute paths and git identity. Never committed.
CLAUDE.local.md

# Editor / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# Generated by "Create Bases dashboards". Output, not fixture: committing them
# would make a fresh clone hit the already-exists path instead of the one that
# actually needs testing.
**/90 Dashboards/*.base

# NOT the same for `_config/reports/`, deliberately. The report templates that
# "Write the built-in report templates to _config" copies out are committed, as
# worked examples: that folder is the first thing anyone edits, and the block
# vocabulary is otherwise documented only in the source of a plugin the work
# laptop cannot build. They are exactly what the command writes, and a fixture
# test holds them equal to the built-ins so an example cannot teach a shape the
# code no longer has. The two paths that lose a live exercise in the test vault
# are still covered: "five templates with nothing in `_config`" by the smoke
# test against an empty vault, and "nothing was overwritten" is now what a
# fresh clone hits.

# Frozen policy revisions and the impact reports beside them: output, not
# fixture, on the same argument as the `.base` files. The test vault ships the
# *inputs* — a policy at version 3 and the reissued document in `_incoming/` —
# so a fresh clone hits the path that actually needs testing: freeze, diff,
# impact map, all written for real. Committing a frozen copy would also mean
# the second run of the demo lands on the name-already-taken branch instead.
**/40 Policies/_revisions/

# Same argument for the daily briefing: it is a dated record the plugin writes,
# not a worked example of the contract. Committing one would also mean a test
# run in the test vault dirties the tree every morning.
**/90 Dashboards/Briefings/

# Mail files. A `.eml` or `.msg` anywhere in a working tree is assumed to be a
# real message dragged out of a real mailbox for testing, so none may be
# committed. This costs nothing: gitignore does not affect already-tracked
# files, so the two synthetic fixtures in `75 Correspondence-fixtures/` stay
# tracked and diffable, and a genuinely new synthetic fixture needs a
# deliberate `git add -f` — which is the point.
*.eml
*.msg
